00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.320
for your data access and audit
logs across all the Cloud

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.990
products and even other clouds.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:20.150
Stackdriver can integrate
with other clouds as well.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.303
We also have OS Login.

00:05:22.303 --> 00:05:24.220
If you're not familiar
with that feature, that

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.640
lets you take a
Cloud IAM Identity,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.860
translate that
into a Linux user,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.900
and the big benefit
there is that you

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.450
don't have to be handing out
and managing SSH keys to get

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:40.520
into your virtual machines.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.967
We also have VPC
Service Controls

00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:45.550
which is going to
give you the ability

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.092
to do a lot of the
things that you're

00:05:47.092 --> 00:05:51.120
doing in your on-prem networking
stack in Google Cloud.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.150
And then we also have Cloud
Identity and Access Management

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.160
as well as Kerberos
support in Dataproc.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.785
And I'm going to talk
a little bit more

00:05:57.785 --> 00:05:59.620
about those in a minute.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.400
But before I do, I want to come
back to some of the data lake

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.330
commonalities that I'm seeing.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.980
So I think between
the three of us,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.820
I think we've seen countless
data lake implementations

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:13.070
from a variety of industries.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.570
And at least me,
personally, I don't

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.672
think I've ever seen
two that were alike.

00:06:16.672 --> 00:06:18.630
Have you guys ever seen
two customer data lakes

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:19.630
that were exactly alike?

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.160
No.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:24.290
But there are three
common elements

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:26.750
that every data lake
has got to address,

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:30.920
ingestion, storage, and
then a compute platform

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.860
that's going to let you use
a variety of tools to mine

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.270
and identify workflows.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.090
So from my perspective,
at least, ingest,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.090
that tends to be
all over the place.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:43.340
Data is now coming in
from just everywhere,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.920
all sorts of volumes,
all sorts of velocities.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.590
And even like in
my GCP world, I've

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.740
seen a lot of common tools,
but a universal pattern

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.492
I've not seen for ingest yet.

00:06:54.492 --> 00:06:56.450
However, when it comes
to number 2 and number 3

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:58.190
where you store
the data and then

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:03.200
having a platform
for tools, customers

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.440
do tend to answer
these in the same way.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.740
So in Cloud Storage, at
least in Google Cloud,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.110
is the store for the data lake.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:18.490
It's the hub that brings
together GCP around your data.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.250
It's going to check that
box for most of those

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.690
features that a
data lake storage

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.290
system is going to require.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.980
You're going to have
high durability,

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.670
centralized security
controls, storage classes that

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.620
help you optimize cost.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.160
And one very relevant
for Hadoop and Spark

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.020
is we have immediate
consistency.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.870
Now that's a really
important feature

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.760
that allows you to
use Cloud Storage

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.587
as a drop-in
replacement for HDFS

00:07:45.587 --> 00:07:47.170
without adding a
bunch of workarounds,

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:50.070
like NoSQL databases.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.890
And so once customers
land on Cloud Storage

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.830
and they've identified that
as their storage mechanism,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.630
the next step is
typically trying

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:01.290
to identify a way to manage
and mine and identify

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.570
all those various workflows.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:06.730
More often than not,
customers always

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:10.480
choose a platform over
a specific tool that

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.910
lets them run a variety
of open-source processing

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.130
applications.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:17.470
You really need a platform
that can provide lots

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.200
of and the latest capabilities.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.530
So Cloudera and Cloud Dataproc
both provide these platforms.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.630
Cloudera is focused on building
a unified, hybrid cloud.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:32.500
Where Cloud Dataproc, we tend
to want to modernize your stack

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.350
and pull you into the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.140
Yet because both
of us are working

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:42.490
on so many of the
similar capabilities

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.050
and in the
open-source community,

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:48.370
we were able to come together
and build some common security

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.550
infrastructure that
we both could use.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:53.290
So in a bit, Larry
and Julien are

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.650
going to talk a little bit
more about that collaboration.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:59.560
But before I hand
off to them, I want

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:05.610
to leave you with some IAM
best practices for data lakes.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.960
So just to backup and
just baseline Cloud IAM,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:15.170
it is who can do what
on which resources.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.530
So you're typically going
to have a project owner.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.600
They'll invite
members to projects.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.580
Then they're going
to grant them roles.

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.380
And then those
roles, those consist

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:28.840
of a collection of permissions
for one or more resources.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.300
Some basic IAM
best practices, you

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:35.950
want to definitely follow the
principle of least privilege.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.220
That applies to identities,
roles, and resources.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.990
So you always want to
select the smallest

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:44.638
scope necessary to reduce
your exposure to risk.

00:09:44.638 --> 00:09:46.180
So you're not going
to grow and grant

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:50.200
everybody like an owner role
to your entire organization.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.030
There's intentional
hacks or mistakes, that

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:53.860
could bring down applications.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.620
You want to be really
specific and deliberate

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:00.400
and assign a specific group,
like a security admin role,

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:06.200
to manage SSL certs and
firewalls on specific projects.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.750
And you want to use groups
as much as possible.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.140
So dealing with
managing permissions

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:14.970
for individual users, that
can be really cumbersome.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.290
It can be error-prone.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:17.720
So you want to use groups.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.580
You can see in this
example, we have

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:22.187
users that are getting added
to a SecOps team group.

00:10:22.187 --> 00:10:24.770
And then it's that group that's
going to get your permissions,

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.763
like security admin
and log viewer.

00:10:29.763 --> 00:10:32.180
You also want to make sure
that you have policies in place

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:33.860
to secure your resources.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.780
You want to make sure
you're controlling

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.860
how additional users gain access
to resources through policies

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:41.220
and group memberships.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.670
Because without strict
control over those policies,

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:46.160
you might be inadvertently
allowing new users

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:47.780
more permission than they need.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.280
And then now we're
back to violating

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.490
that principle of least policy.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:52.130
OK.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.948
At this point, maybe a lot
of you are rolling your eyes

00:10:54.948 --> 00:10:56.990
or pulling out your phones
thinking, yeah, Chris,

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:57.740
I get this.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.670
This is every RBAC system ever.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.770
And there's some
validity to that.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.910
But here's some
important things to keep

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.630
in mind that are actually
different in Google Cloud.

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.180
So you're going to have two
members of your data lake

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:12.320
in Google Cloud.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.210
You're going to have
the accounts that

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.490
might be either a Gmail
or a Google address.

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:19.130
Most likely, you're going
to have folks coming

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:21.200
from your G Suite domain.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.410
So that's going to be the
folks that have an @ and then

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.140
your company's name dot com.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:31.640
It's important to understand
that GCP does not create

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.300
or manage users or groups.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:35.800
I'm going to say
that one more time.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:41.480
GCP does not create or
manage users or groups.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:45.910
So the "I" in our IAM, that's
almost a bit of a misnomer

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:47.200
sometimes.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.710
When you assign a permission
to someone in Cloud IAM,

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.120
it's going onto that user.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.960
But that user lives
outside of the GCP project.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.130
And they might be associated
with many GCP projects or even

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:02.530
a different organization
altogether with a different set

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:03.970
of permissions.

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:06.760
That's why in GCP
in your data lake,

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:09.520
it is really important
to scope things

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.790
as much as possible
with service accounts

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:15.970
because those service accounts
are associated and limited

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:18.980
to those GCP projects.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:22.990
So now that we understand
that users and groups are not

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.730
part of your specific
GCP projects,

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:27.610
here are some data
lake best practices

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.330
that may not be quite as
intuitive as that first set.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:36.670
So your data lake objects,
your GCS bucket ACLs,

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:41.953
those default ACLs, any object
ACLs as much as possible,

00:12:41.953 --> 00:12:43.120
those should contain groups.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:43.620
Great.

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:44.830
We established that.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:47.650
But those groups,
as much as possible,

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:51.550
should contain IAM
service accounts.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:53.470
You want those service
accounts, then,

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:56.603
to represent a type
of automated workload.

00:12:56.603 --> 00:12:58.270
And then what you're
going to want to do

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:02.680
is grant end users the
iam.serviceAccountUser role

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:06.320
to that service account.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.020
You also, as much as
possible, the goal

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.120
here is we want
to avoid exposing

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.730
a long-live credential.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:17.410
Really avoid that if that
credential is associated

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:21.340
with a real end user
because remember, that user,

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.380
they might have other
permissions that are not

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:25.450
part of your GCP project.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.130
So let's say I'm
working with Larry.

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.050
And he's from Cloudera.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.290
He only has some read-only, the
identified access to my data.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:33.290
I might think, whatever.

00:13:33.290 --> 00:13:34.570
I'll give him a
long-lived credential

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.760
because it doesn't really
matter in my project.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:41.320
But if it is with his real
user, he also probably

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.190
has access to potentially other
Cloudera-- in other Cloudera

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.180
area projects, he
could be an admin.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.120
And so creating these
long-lived credentials

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.258
for real people, that creates
almost like a somewhat

00:13:52.258 --> 00:13:54.550
ambiguous blast radius,
because you're not just limited

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.620
to the GCP projects
that you have

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:59.860
control over for these users.

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:03.400
And so also as much as
possible, try to use

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.680
the Google APIs,
things like the GCloud,

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.870
or if you're using
Cloud Dataproc,

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:11.810
use the Jobs API to break apart
isolation and permissions.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.340
So the Cloud Dataproc
Jobs API, that

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.980
will determine who
can submit jobs

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:19.000
to the cluster versus who has
actual access to the cluster.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.480
And better yet is even
if you can do isolation

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:27.420
based on the VM or
the cluster itself.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.720
And so I know I just said
that's my best practice,

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.730
is isolate by VM or cluster.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:35.220
But I'm also very well
aware that that is not

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.550
practical in reality for
a lot of situations, which

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.580
is exactly why we do have
a beta version of Kerberos

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.207
now integrated with Dataproc.

00:14:43.207 --> 00:14:44.790
And this is going
to let you do things

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:48.180
like tie back your Dataproc
users to Active Directory.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.310
It also makes it as
simple as a button click

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.550
to turn on security features
like Hadoop secure mode

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.860
or encryption in-flight.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.800
Now, the standard model
in this Kerberos beta

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.380
is that users access
Cloud Dataproc.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.787
They're going to obtain a
Kerberos principal for the work

00:15:03.787 --> 00:15:06.330
that they do on
the cluster itself.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:10.630
And then everything done on the
cluster is that Kerberos user.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:13.320
However, if the data does need
to get accessed from the data

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.190
lake in Google Cloud
Storage, that data

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.590
is going to get obtained
using the service account

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:23.735
credentials of the virtual
machines of the cluster.

00:15:23.735 --> 00:15:25.110
Now, this model
works really well

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.410
for customers that can lock down
their Cloud Storage buckets.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:31.060
And this is really what
a lot of customers want.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.570
They want all the security
controls in their cluster.

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:37.080
That lets them use tools
like Apache Ranger.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.020
However, we do have
a lot of customers

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:43.980
with very stringent security
and compliance requirements

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:47.670
that actually need to translate
that identity, that Kerberos

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:51.510
identity back into a Cloud
identity so the chain of access

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:53.850
never gets broken.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:55.980
This is actually
a security problem

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:59.400
that exists across other
cloud providers as well.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.280
And it was something
that Google and Cloudera

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.318
were both working to address.

00:16:04.318 --> 00:16:06.360
And so I think you now
know where this is headed.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:07.777
I'm going to hand
off to Larry now

00:16:07.777 --> 00:16:10.590
to talk about how Google
and Cloudera came together

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.610
to solve that dilemma.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:14.708
LARRY MCCAY: OK.

00:16:14.708 --> 00:16:16.250
What Chris was
talking about just now

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:19.245
is something that I've been
calling the cloud identity

00:16:19.245 --> 00:16:19.745
crisis.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:26.330
And we'll dig into
what that means.

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:30.770
So challenges for
data lake identity

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:35.330
when we're in the cloud or we're
accessing cloud data, something

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.060
I call the identity gap.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:40.880
We'll get into each
of these things.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.190
Related to this, we end
up with a phenomenon

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:48.650
called credential
sprawl and also

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:52.100
this issue of being able to use
short-lived credentials rather

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:53.628
than long-lived credentials.

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:01.290
So this gray area on this slide
represents this identity gap.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:05.490
And on one side, you have
the Hadoop and Kerberos

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:11.849
users or your Enterprise users
which Hadoop knows about.

00:17:11.849 --> 00:17:15.210
And the other side, you have
your client Cloud IAM users

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:19.300
and identities, which
Cloud Storage knows about.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.050
But Cloud Storage doesn't
know about Kerberos users.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:25.740
And Hadoop doesn't
know about IAM users.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:32.970
So we've actually supported
these cloud data connectors

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:34.000
for a number of years.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.490
And we've had to
bridge this gap.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.880
So these don't really
represent points in time.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.850
But these are
different approaches

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.100
that have been used to
try to bridge this gap.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.360
And they're all some flavor
of Bring Your Own Keys.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:57.120
So we started out using
environment variables.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.150
We've had username and
password in the URL

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:03.450
itself to have your keys.

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:07.140
We have configuration
files where

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:09.990
you put your keys in there.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:12.990
We have instance profile
or service accounts

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:17.720
associated with the VM,
which Chris mentioned.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.860
We added the ability to protect
your keys with the Hadoop

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:24.400
CredentialProvider.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:29.520
And coming soon, we have a
delegation token support.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:32.900
So these are all attempts
to kind of bridge that gap.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:36.830
But they led to this
credential sprawl.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.525
So I have many jobs
from many users running.

00:18:43.525 --> 00:18:44.900
And they're
configuring your keys

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.568
across your platform
in various ways.

00:18:47.568 --> 00:18:48.860
How do you know where they are?

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:51.140
How do you know
where they end up?

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.290
They are in-- possibly
in environment variables.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.420
Not often anymore, but could be.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.590
Configuration files.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.170
They could be in
credential stores,

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.680
which are key stores that are
across your data platform.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:10.430
They could end up in process
listings in log files.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:15.460
They could end up in
GitHub repositories.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.970
So this is what I'm
calling credential sprawl.

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:20.500
It's hard to find them.

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:22.990
It's hard to clean them up.

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:25.750
And it's very easy to leak them.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.930
So we need a way
to address that.

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:31.180
If you notice, I'm not saying
anything about number 5

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:32.192
here yet.

00:19:32.192 --> 00:19:35.560
This is related to the work
that we've come together

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:38.251
to work on and collaborate on.

00:19:38.251 --> 00:19:45.470
And this introduces the use
of delegation tokens, which

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:48.290
is a native part of Hadoop.

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:51.080
And this is the standard
way that credentials

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:54.230
are propagated across
your data platform for use

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.830
by worker tasks that
don't have Kerberos

00:19:57.830 --> 00:20:02.010
credentials of their own
possibly and things like that.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.290
They are securely
distributed with the jobs

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.780
and cleaned up when
the jobs are done.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:10.420
They're also time-constrained
with an expiration time

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:12.970
and a fixed number of times
that they could be renewed

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:17.080
within another time period.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:19.360
And very importantly,
they're already

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:23.750
supported in the
existing Hadoop clients.

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:28.780
So in order to introduce a
new delegation token type

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:32.170
or to introduce new sources
of delegation tokens,

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:36.190
you don't have to change
any of the Hadoop clients.

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:42.462
So this can also be leveraged
to broker tokens, which

00:20:42.462 --> 00:20:43.420
we'll talk about later.

00:20:49.615 --> 00:20:51.240
So this picture--
I'm not going to talk

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.820
in great detail about this
because Julien actually

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:57.150
has a nice presentation
of this overall flow.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:01.140
But this is the
canonical picture

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.750
of how delegation
tokens are actually

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:08.220
used for accessing HDFS.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:13.940
So a user logs in,
and they submit a job.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:17.700
The Hadoop client
interrogates the file systems

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.370
that are being
used and tells them

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:22.590
to go collect their
delegation tokens, which

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:31.100
then get distributed
to the worker nodes

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:34.860
and are available to those
tasks while they're running.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:40.670
And upon job completion,
they're all cleaned up.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:46.527
So that's essentially
Kerberos for HDFS.

00:21:46.527 --> 00:21:47.110
Remember that.

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.690
So open source, open source is
actually why I'm on the stage

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:54.470
here.

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.990
It's actually why I
joined Hortonworks

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:59.570
when I joined
Hortonworks and why

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:02.570
I'm happy to still be
with the new Cloudera

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:04.430
to work in open source.

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:14.420
So contributors to open source
not only dedicate their time

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:16.860
and commit their time
to these projects,

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.620
but at the end of the day,
they put their name on it.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:21.350
So I want to call
out some people who

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:24.590
have worked on this stuff
because this is moving

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:27.140
the platform forward actually.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:31.190
So Steve Loughran, who
happens to work for Cloudera,

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:35.570
created this GRN,
this patch, which

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:39.770
added delegation token
support to S3A, which is

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:42.530
a data connector for S3 access.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.340
He also provided an out
of the box implementation

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:53.940
for those delegation tokens to
limit that credential sprawl.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:23:00.010
So you can now use your
keys from your desktop only,

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:04.240
so authenticate the
AWS, and submit a job.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:09.670
And his change, we'll
call AWS STS service,

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:13.440
and acquire a short-lived
session tokens where you.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:18.000
And they get distributed as
delegation tokens with the jobs

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.650
and cleaned up when
the job's complete.

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:22.890
They are short-lived tokens.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:25.380
And there's no really
way to renew them.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:29.560
But it really minimizes this
credential sprawl phenomenon

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:33.555
to your desktop where
you're using them.

00:23:33.555 --> 00:23:34.930
So this was a
great step forward.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.240
So as we looked
at that mechanism

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:48.260
and we realized
with minimal change,

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:53.310
we can swap out the
source of these tokens,

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:57.710
we realized we can
start normalizing

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.650
how tokens are accessed
across all of the cloud vendor

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:02.180
connectors.

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:05.810
So Phil Zampino,
also from Cloudera,

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:08.690
contributed a patch
back to the GCS data

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:11.480
connector in open source.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:15.380
And Julien, Igor, and Chao
reviewed and collaborated

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:20.180
and improved this
patch so that it

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:28.200
works as well as the S3A patch
did and meets the same needs.

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:35.840
So that brings me to the
Cloudera Identity Broker

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:38.150
And this is an upcoming feature.

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:39.440
It is not released yet.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.170
The goals, essentially,
if we were to boil

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:47.810
it down is remember, I
say, Kerberos for HDFS.

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:49.625
This is Kerberos for the cloud.

00:24:49.625 --> 00:24:52.040
It's the easiest
way to think of it.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:53.960
You log in with your
Enterprise credentials

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.310
and KN it as normally.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:03.000
Delegation tokens are
acquired from the IDBroker

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.970
and then later used to acquire
access tokens as needed.

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.060
The goals for the
IDBroker itself

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:15.090
are a secure multi-user data
lake deployment in the cloud.

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:18.917
And its multicloud
and hybrid cloud.

00:25:18.917 --> 00:25:21.000
Like I said, you authenticate
with your Enterprise

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:22.680
credentials.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:27.420
We only use
short-term IAM tokens.

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:30.150
And we leverage your
existing investment

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:34.030
in Cloud IAM policies
and tooling, et cetera.

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.870
We also have the ability to
renew those short-term tokens.

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:40.800
So long-lived jobs will
still maintain their access.

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:48.180
So this is a simple picture of
the existing ecosystem of data

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.120
connectors along with HDFS.

00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:58.420
And these red credentials needed
represents that identity gap

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:00.240
we were talking about before.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:07.000
And all those many ways of
filling that gap were used.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:10.440
But this is the picture
we're moving towards now.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:14.130
So all the existing
clients are going

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:15.630
to be talking to
the data connectors

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:19.800
and telling them to go get their
delegation tokens transparently

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:20.370
to the user.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:22.800
They're getting acquired
from the identity broker.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.340
And access tokens are
acquired and ready for access

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:27.300
to Cloud Storage.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:36.080
So this is essentially
Kerberos for the Cloud.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:40.030
The same picture is
Kerberos for HDFS.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:41.890
But it shows how
the Identity Broker

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:46.600
is used for acquiring delegation
tokens and access tokens

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:48.580
and then subsequently
providing access

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.980
to Cloud Storage from
the worker nodes.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:59.043
So in order for us to have--

00:26:59.043 --> 00:27:00.710
and one of our
requirements was actually

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:04.700
to not require IAM users for
every one of your Enterprise

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:06.350
users.

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:11.420
In order to not do that, we
need a way to associate "roles"

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:12.080
quote unquote.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:18.420
In S3, they are
actually IAM roles.

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:23.010
In GCP, they are
service accounts.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:30.090
So we associate group
memberships to roles.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.940
And we have a role
selection algorithm

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:36.300
that picks the best match
based on configuration

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.250
based on these
mappings and based

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:40.030
on a number of other things.

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:43.020
And this is just
a mockup of what

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:44.520
a UI will look like to do this.

00:27:47.943 --> 00:27:53.250
So kind of just to
illustrate the power of this,

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:56.100
of being able to just use
your Enterprise credentials,

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:57.930
we'll walk through
a simple use case

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:02.240
of migrating data from one cloud
provider to another using TCP.

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:14.160
So you log in with your
typical Kerberos kinit.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.690
And you issue the
Hadoop distcp command

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:21.490
with your source and
your destination URLs.

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:23.470
And you can see by
the scheme there

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:28.150
that one is for S3 and
one is for Google Storage.

00:28:32.530 --> 00:28:34.870
So the client, as
I described before,

00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:37.960
iterates over the file
systems it's about to use

00:28:37.960 --> 00:28:41.950
and tells it to collect
its delegation tokens.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:46.810
The delegation tokens are then
collected from the IDBroker

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:48.550
for both of those file systems.

00:28:53.778 --> 00:28:55.695
They're collected into
the App Launch Context.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:03.890
And you can see one for each
of the cloud storage vendors

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:07.280
there as well as in HDFS,
the delegation token here.

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:15.080
And YARN, it
distributes it along

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:17.390
with the App Launch
Context and the credentials

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:18.710
in a secure manner.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:20.870
And during execution,
those credentials

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:25.670
are used to move the data
from the source bucket

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:27.380
to the destination bucket.

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:31.130
And the way that it does that
is it takes the delegation token

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.868
and it authenticates
the IDBroker.

00:29:32.868 --> 00:29:34.910
Even though it doesn't
have Kerberos credentials,

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.280
it's using that
delegation token.

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:40.190
And it's requesting
access tokens.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:50.080
It will also ask for new
access tokens when they expire.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:53.110
And then as the job
completes, delegation tokens

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.730
are all cleaned up.

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:57.580
And we eliminate credential
sprawl completely.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.040
We logged in with our
Enterprise credentials.

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:03.250
And we ended up dynamically
getting access tokens

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:08.020
for as long as we needed, even
though they're short-lived,

00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:10.450
from multiple cloud vendors.

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:12.660
And your data is now migrated.

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:16.570
So just kind of a
summary slide here.

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:21.970
We have multicloud access token
acquisition for AWS, S3, Google

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:23.390
GCS.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:31.000
Azure ADLS Gen2 is
still being worked on.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.760
You only need your Enterprise
credentials as I said.

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:37.690
Supports long-lived jobs
with short-lived tokens

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:43.250
and leverages the existing
support in Hadoop clients.

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:44.800
Another thing to
be aware of, which

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:48.190
is a related but
separate effort,

00:30:48.190 --> 00:30:53.560
is that we are working on
common Enterprise data policy

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:57.170
across on-prem and Cloud
Storage data access.

00:30:57.170 --> 00:30:58.900
So that's also coming soon.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:09.090
So I will hand it
over now to Julien

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:12.990
who will talk about the GCP
Token Broker, which was enabled

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:16.680
by the same collaboration.

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:18.176
JULIEN PHALIP: Thank you, Larry.

00:31:18.176 --> 00:31:19.940
And hi, everyone.

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:21.100
I'm Julien Phalip.

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:23.070
I'm a solutions architect
at Google Cloud.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:24.990
And I'm really excited
to introduce you

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:26.640
to the GCP Token Broker.

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:32.960
So what is the GCP Token Broker?

00:31:32.960 --> 00:31:35.030
It is a brand new
open-source project

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:37.010
that we just released last week.

00:31:37.010 --> 00:31:38.635
It currently is in alpha.

00:31:38.635 --> 00:31:40.010
And I will talk
to you a bit more

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:43.070
about the upcoming roadmap.

00:31:43.070 --> 00:31:45.260
So this is the result
of a close collaboration

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:47.570
that we've had with several
large Hadoop customers

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:52.190
as well as with partners such
as Cloudera and Hortonworks.

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:54.500
The project on GitHub
contains documentation

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:58.280
about the design
that we've aligned on

00:31:58.280 --> 00:32:02.070
as well as reference
implementation.

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:06.410
So this project is intended
for Enterprise customers

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:08.990
who are currently using
Kerberos to secure

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:14.190
their on-prem clusters
and who are looking

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:17.220
to migrate those
clusters to the Cloud

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:25.190
and try to replicate the same
user workflows and experience.

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:28.210
So this is particularly
valuable for customers

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:31.510
who are interested in a hybrid
environment where they can

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:34.870
let the users run jobs
on-prem as well as run jobs

00:32:34.870 --> 00:32:35.460
in the Cloud.

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:41.080
So the main goal for
the GCP Token Broker

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:44.030
is to ease that migration.

00:32:44.030 --> 00:32:48.140
And this is done by leveraging
the same Kerberos and Hadoop

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:52.050
delegation token facility
that Larry introduced to.

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:59.620
This facility allows
to securely distribute

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:03.010
short-lived credentials
across a cluster.

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:05.970
It also allows for a
seamless integration

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:08.580
across multiple
tools and frameworks

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:10.410
around the Hadoop ecosystem.

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.100
That includes things like
Spark, Hive, Tez, YARN,

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:17.170
MapReduce, and many others.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:22.300
And because the
integration is so seamless,

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:23.950
this reduces the
changes that you

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:28.240
have to make when you're
migrating your clusters.

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:32.450
The Broker also helps
enforce AAA security.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:34.510
This means that users
can continue to use

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:37.600
Kerberos to authenticate.

00:33:37.600 --> 00:33:40.630
And you can also have a
very granular authorization

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:43.510
where you can set permission
on individual users

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:47.010
for specific buckets.

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.560
And as far as
accounting goes, these

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:52.560
are also allows you to do
some very granular access

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:57.180
logs, which is very useful for
troubleshooting, monitoring,

00:33:57.180 --> 00:34:01.860
or detailed chargebacks where
you can trace back which user

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:05.130
actually used which
bucket or which resources

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:08.370
in the Cloud at what time.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:11.810
And the Broker offers
tight integration

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:16.159
with the Google Cloud
Services, starting

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:19.710
with Cloud Storage in the
future with other services,

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:23.139
such as BigQuery or Bigtable.

00:34:23.139 --> 00:34:25.929
So while the IDBroker
that Larry talked to

00:34:25.929 --> 00:34:30.460
is focused on the
Cloudera environment

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:37.130
and to allow
multicloud jobs to run,

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:40.580
the Broker currently is
focused on the Google Cloud

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:43.790
environment.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:47.600
Now looking at the
workflow at a high level,

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:49.018
again, this should
look familiar.

00:34:49.018 --> 00:34:50.560
It's trying to solve
the same problem

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:52.340
that Larry described earlier.

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:55.239
So you have your users,
your Kerberos principals,

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:58.860
who want to use a Hadoop
environment to run jobs.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:00.360
And as part of those
jobs, they want

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:04.350
to also be able to access Cloud
resources like Cloud Storage.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.930
Now, Cloud Storage, like
all the Cloud services,

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:11.490
work with Cloud Identities.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:15.230
So what do you want
to achieve basically

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:18.950
is you want to let your users
to continue using Kerberos

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:21.960
to authenticate and
run jobs and some way,

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:26.090
somehow match those identities
with Cloud Identities

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:30.645
so that they can then
access Cloud services.

00:35:30.645 --> 00:35:33.020
Now let's zoom in a little
bit in that Hadoop environment

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:35.550
to see how it all works.

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:41.070
So now the system revolves
around this Broker service.

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.720
There is a prerequisite
that you need

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:48.850
to set up first, that is to use
Google Cloud Directory Sync.

00:35:48.850 --> 00:35:51.520
It's a tool that's been
around for a long time.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:56.350
What it does it will sync your
LDAP on-prem identities over

00:35:56.350 --> 00:35:58.010
to Cloud Identities.

00:35:58.010 --> 00:35:59.310
This is done automatically.

00:35:59.310 --> 00:36:02.450
It will sync all of
your users and groups.

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:07.210
Once you have all of your
identities synced in the Cloud,

00:36:07.210 --> 00:36:11.650
you can then write standard
IAM policies for those users

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:14.320
and groups and dictate what
those users and groups are

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:15.520
allowed to access.

00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:17.200
In a Hadoop context,
typically, this

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:21.880
would mean setting
permissions on GCS buckets.

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:24.880
And then once that's
in place, your users

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:28.450
are ready to submit jobs.

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:32.820
So here is Alice, a
Kerberos principal.

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:35.730
What Alice will do
first is to log in

00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:41.330
by running the kinit
command with the KDC.

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:45.840
The KDC is backed by
your LDAP database.

00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:47.580
And also, very
importantly, the Broker

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:50.520
is also connected
to the same KDC

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:53.850
so that it will be able to
authenticate your users further

00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:56.780
along in the process.

00:36:56.780 --> 00:36:59.570
Then now that
Alice is logged in,

00:36:59.570 --> 00:37:02.570
she can submit a job by
using a Hadoop client.

00:37:02.570 --> 00:37:04.280
First thing that
the client does is

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:09.200
to contact the broker to ask
for new delegation token.

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:11.150
The Broker at this
point uses Kerberos

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:13.790
to authenticate
the user, make sure

00:37:13.790 --> 00:37:16.310
that that user is
who they claim to be

00:37:16.310 --> 00:37:19.500
and then generates a new
access token and return that--

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:23.510
sorry, a delegation token and
return the delegation token

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:25.130
to the client.

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:28.250
The client then submits a job
to the YARN Resource Manager

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:31.838
and passes along that
delegation token.

00:37:31.838 --> 00:37:34.130
The YARN Resource Manager,
the first thing that he does

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:37.460
is to send a renew
request to the Broker just

00:37:37.460 --> 00:37:40.160
to make sure that it has full
authority over that delegation

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.370
token.

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:47.340
And then the job is effectively
submitted across a cluster

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:49.980
over multiple tasks.

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:53.020
As those tasks are running
at some point in time,

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:56.310
they might need to access Cloud
Storage, whether to read a file

00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:58.680
or write a file.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.950
When a task needs to do that,
the first thing that it will do

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:04.770
is to contact the
Broker again, this time

00:38:04.770 --> 00:38:07.530
by pulling the delegation
token from the secure context

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:10.690
and sending the dedication
token to the Broker.

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:13.440
The Broker then verifies that
the delegation token is still

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:14.800
valid.

00:38:14.800 --> 00:38:21.120
And if so will then call the
Google OAuth API to generate

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:23.850
a new access token
for the Cloud Identity

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:28.310
that maps to the
Kerberos identity.

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:32.330
That access token is then
returned over to the task.

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:39.990
And a task can then access Cloud
Storage as that Cloud Identity,

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:44.140
so Alice, the Cloud Identity.

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:46.700
And then at the end,
when, the job is complete

00:38:46.700 --> 00:38:50.840
the YARN Resource Manager will
call the Broker one last time

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.390
and call the cancel on point.

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:59.030
This will effectively make the
delegation token inoperable

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:02.730
so that it cannot be reused
outside of the context of this

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:03.230
job.

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:11.480
So from the very
beginning, the Token Broker

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:15.080
was designed with
scalability in mind

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:19.880
by using several fully managed
products on Google Cloud.

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:23.270
That includes Kubernetes
Engine, Cloud Datastore,

00:39:23.270 --> 00:39:25.460
Cloud Memorystore, Cloud KMS.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:29.320
Now let's look at how
this all fits together.

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:32.290
What you can see here
in the top-left corner

00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:34.660
is the Broker environment.

00:39:34.660 --> 00:39:38.050
The Broker service is
running on Kubernetes Engine.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:40.240
And then you can
scale that cluster

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:45.010
up to how ever many nodes as
you need to bear the load that's

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.645
required for your users.

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:55.160
The state is stored in a
database on Cloud Datastore,

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:58.610
which is a highly
scalable NoSQL database

00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:01.730
and fully managed
on Google Cloud.

00:40:01.730 --> 00:40:04.430
That state includes the
delegation token and also

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:07.190
some session details that
are attached to the jobs that

00:40:07.190 --> 00:40:08.120
are currently running.

00:40:10.730 --> 00:40:14.480
There is a cache currently
using Redis on Cloud Memorystore

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:18.000
to improve performance.

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:21.370
Cloud KMS is used to
encrypt any sensitive data.

00:40:21.370 --> 00:40:23.670
So for example, before
caching the access token,

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:28.260
those tokens are
encrypted with KMS.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.760
And every operation
done by the Broker

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:32.620
is also logged in Stackdriver.

00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:38.650
So you can monitor how
the Broker is used.

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:41.010
Now looking at the
top-right corner,

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:43.310
this is your client environment.

00:40:43.310 --> 00:40:45.880
This is where your Hadoop
clusters will be running.

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:50.050
And you can choose to run
it either as a self-managed

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:51.550
cluster on Compute Engine.

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:53.590
Or you can also
use Dataproc, which

00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:59.680
is Google's fully managed
Hadoop and Spark service.

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:04.270
A couple of configuration
setup that you have to do,

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:06.310
first, you need to
set up a two-way trust

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:09.610
between the client machines
and the Broker service.

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:13.030
This is to allow
services like YARN that

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:15.640
are running on the current
machines to access the Broker

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:19.240
and also to allow
logged in Kerberos users

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:21.640
to also access the Broker.

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:25.120
And then you also need
to set up a one-way trust

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:28.300
between the Broker
and the LDAP database.

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:32.710
This is so that the Broker
can authenticate your users.

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:35.220
And then you can
have your data be

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:39.970
hosted on any number
of buckets as you need.

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:46.040
What you can see here with the
dotted lines are separate VPCs.

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:49.180
All of those VPCs
are private VPCs.

00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:52.180
All of those machines
only use private IPs.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:54.910
And you can then interconnect
those VPCs in different ways,

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:59.790
by using VPC sharing,
VPC peering, or VPNs.

00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:03.960
So this, I want to emphasize
is only an example deployment.

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:07.620
The project on Github
contains Terraform scripts

00:42:07.620 --> 00:42:10.710
that will allow you to
automatically deploy

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:12.540
this architecture.

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:15.510
But you should just use
that as a starting point

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:18.510
and adapt it potentially to
your production environment

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:19.360
as you see fit.

00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:27.800
And then you can also swap
out any of those components.

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:30.250
So if you choose to
use, for example, MySQL,

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:33.280
a MySQL database
to store the state,

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:37.350
you can totally do that and
replace Datastore, for example.

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:39.320
Or you can also use
Memcached instead of Redis.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:44.290
Quick word on the road map.

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:47.140
So this current FR
release contains support

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:50.250
for Cloud Storage
and also supports

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:54.790
Kerberos as the authentication
backend and also supports

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:59.890
proxy users, which is a Hadoop
concept that allows services

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:04.240
like Hive, for example, to
impersonate other users.

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:06.070
In the upcoming
beta, we're going

00:43:06.070 --> 00:43:08.590
to focus on performance
optimizations

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:11.850
and on stabilizing the API.

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:18.050
And then if-- sorry,
in future releases,

00:43:18.050 --> 00:43:21.620
we will add several more
features most prominently

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:25.370
by adding support for
BigQuery and Bigtable.

00:43:25.370 --> 00:43:28.700
So I invite you to look
at the project on Github

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:32.240
for more details about
the design, the roadmap,

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:34.250
as well as the actual
implementation that you

00:43:34.250 --> 00:43:38.520
can then try out and
use in your environment.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:41.450
We would really appreciate
a good feedback.

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:47.590
We want to make sure that we
can implement all the features

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:49.180
that you want and need.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:51.760
So please feel
free to try it out.

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:55.208
And get in touch with
your sales team at Google.

00:43:55.208 --> 00:43:57.250
And then we'll be sure to
follow up and make sure

00:43:57.250 --> 00:43:59.210
that we iterate and
make it better for you.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:04.390
So yeah, thank you very much.

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:06.320
This is the end of the talk.

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:09.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

